prompt:
You are implementing a small system from a product brief. Work only in the current directory.

# The brief

# Order lifecycle

A shop keeps orders. Each order is named by the caller with a string id. An order is created empty, filled with items, placed, paid for, and shipped, and it can be cancelled on the way. Placing an order asks the payment provider to charge the customer, through the effect port, and the provider's answer arrives later as the completion of that effect. Cancelling an order that was paid for asks the provider to refund the customer; the refund is counted, and needs no answer.

## Commands

Each command returns `{ ok: true }` when it takes effect, and `{ ok: false, reason }` when it is refused. A refused command changes nothing. Any command on an order id that was never created is refused.

- `create(order)`: creates the order, empty, as a **draft**. Refused if an order with that id exists.
- `addItem(order)`: adds one item to a draft. Refused unless the order is a draft, and refused when the draft already holds 3 items.
- `removeItem(order)`: removes one item from a draft. Refused unless the order is a draft holding at least one item.
- `place(order)`: places a draft that holds at least one item. The order becomes **placed** and one charge is requested from the payment provider: the effect `payment.charge` with data `{ amount }`, the number of items. Refused otherwise.
- `ship(order)`: a paid order becomes **shipped**. Refused otherwise.
- `cancel(order)`: a draft becomes **cancelled**. A paid order becomes **cancelled** and one refund is requested from the payment provider. Refused while the order is placed (the payment is in progress), and refused once it is shipped, failed or cancelled.

## The charge

A charge is completed through the effect port, by `completeEffect(id, outcome)` on the id `pendingEffects()` lists for it, with `from` the order id. When it completes `ok`, the charge went through: a placed order becomes **paid**. When it completes `failed`, a placed order becomes **failed**. A completed effect is no longer pending; an answer that finds the order no longer placed changes nothing.

## Query

`order(order)` returns `{ status, items, charges, refunds }` for an order that exists, and `null` otherwise:

- `status`: one of `"draft"`, `"placed"`, `"paid"`, `"shipped"`, `"cancelled"`, `"failed"`;
- `items`: the number of items, 0 to 3;
- `charges`: how many charges were requested for the order;
- `refunds`: how many refunds were requested for the order.

## The interface

Implement `host.ts`. Its default export creates a fresh system, with no orders and no effect pending.

## Conclusion

An order is charged at most once, only when it is placed with at least one item, and refunded exactly when it is cancelled after being paid. It ships only when paid, and a shipped, failed or cancelled order accepts no further command.

# The host interface

Your implementation must satisfy this TypeScript interface, which is also in `host.ts`. Hidden tests will call it, and only it, to judge your work: they create a fresh system with the module's default export for every test.

```ts
// T1, order lifecycle: the host interface every arm implements (PT13).

/** The outcome of a command: it took effect, or it was refused and changed nothing. */
export type Outcome = { ok: true } | { ok: false; reason: string };

/** An effect the system asked the outside world to carry out, not yet completed. */
export interface Effect {
  id: string;
  /** The order that requested it. */
  from: string;
  kind: string;
  data: Record<string, unknown>;
}

/** The effect port: what the payment provider is asked to do, and its answers. */
export interface Effects {
  /** Every effect requested and not yet completed, in no promised order. */
  pendingEffects(): Promise<Effect[]>;
  /** The provider's answer to a pending effect: it went through (ok) or it did not (failed). */
  completeEffect(id: string, outcome: 'ok' | 'failed'): Promise<void>;
}

export interface OrderView {
  status: 'draft' | 'placed' | 'paid' | 'shipped' | 'cancelled' | 'failed';
  items: number;
  charges: number;
  refunds: number;
}

export interface Host extends Effects {
  create(order: string): Promise<Outcome>;
  addItem(order: string): Promise<Outcome>;
  removeItem(order: string): Promise<Outcome>;
  place(order: string): Promise<Outcome>;
  ship(order: string): Promise<Outcome>;
  cancel(order: string): Promise<Outcome>;
  /** The order, or null when no order has that id. */
  order(order: string): Promise<OrderView | null>;
}

/** The module's default export: a factory of a fresh system, with no orders. */
export type CreateHost = () => Promise<Host>;
```

# How to build it

Write the implementation in TypeScript. Node 24 runs `.ts` files directly by stripping their types, so use only syntax that erases to JavaScript: no `enum`, no `namespace`, no parameter properties. `npx tsc --noEmit` type-checks your code (`typescript` is installed).

# Your tools

The file tools, and a shell for `node`, `npx tsc` and `npm test`.

# What to deliver

`src/host.impl.ts`, whose default export is the factory of `host.ts` (`CreateHost`): an async function that returns a fresh system. It may import other files under `src/` and Node's built-in modules, and nothing else.

Test your work as you go; you may write any tests you like. When you are done, say so in one line. You will then receive changes to the brief, one at a time, in this same session; each asks you to update the same delivery.

input.content:
export type Outcome = { ok: true } | { ok: false; reason: string };

export interface Effect {
  id: string;
  from: string;
  kind: string;
  data: Record<string, unknown>;
}

type Status = 'draft' | 'placed' | 'paid' | 'shipped' | 'cancelled' | 'failed';

export interface OrderView {
  status: Status;
  items: number;
  charges: number;
  refunds: number;
}

export interface Host {
  pendingEffects(): Promise<Effect[]>;
  completeEffect(id: string, outcome: 'ok' | 'failed'): Promise<void>;
  create(order: string): Promise<Outcome>;
  addItem(order: string): Promise<Outcome>;
  removeItem(order: string): Promise<Outcome>;
  place(order: string): Promise<Outcome>;
  ship(order: string): Promise<Outcome>;
  cancel(order: string): Promise<Outcome>;
  order(order: string): Promise<OrderView | null>;
}

const MAX_ITEMS = 3;

const OK: Outcome = { ok: true };
const refuse = (reason: string): Outcome => ({ ok: false, reason });

export default async function createHost(): Promise<Host> {
  const orders = new Map<string, OrderView>();
  const pending = new Map<string, Effect>();
  let nextEffect = 1;

  const request = (from: string, kind: string, data: Record<string, unknown>): void => {
    const id = `effect-${nextEffect++}`;
    pending.set(id, { id, from, kind, data });
  };

  const withOrder = (id: string, fn: (o: OrderView) => Outcome): Outcome => {
    const o = orders.get(id);
    if (!o) return refuse(`no order ${id}`);
    return fn(o);
  };

  return {
    async create(id) {
      if (orders.has(id)) return refuse(`order ${id} exists`);
      orders.set(id, { status: 'draft', items: 0, charges: 0, refunds: 0 });
      return OK;
    },

    async addItem(id) {
      return withOrder(id, (o) => {
        if (o.status !== 'draft') return refuse(`order is ${o.status}, not draft`);
        if (o.items >= MAX_ITEMS) return refuse(`draft already holds ${MAX_ITEMS} items`);
        o.items++;
        return OK;
      });
    },

    async removeItem(id) {
      return withOrder(id, (o) => {
        if (o.status !== 'draft') return refuse(`order is ${o.status}, not draft`);
        if (o.items < 1) return refuse('draft holds no item');
        o.items--;
        return OK;
      });
    },

    async place(id) {
      return withOrder(id, (o) => {
        if (o.status !== 'draft') return refuse(`order is ${o.status}, not draft`);
        if (o.items < 1) return refuse('draft holds no item');
        o.status = 'placed';
        o.charges++;
        request(id, 'payment.charge', { amount: o.items });
        return OK;
      });
    },

    async ship(id) {
      return withOrder(id, (o) => {
        if (o.status !== 'paid') return refuse(`order is ${o.status}, not paid`);
        o.status = 'shipped';
        return OK;
      });
    },

    async cancel(id) {
      return withOrder(id, (o) => {
        if (o.status === 'draft') {
          o.status = 'cancelled';
          return OK;
        }
        if (o.status === 'paid') {
          o.status = 'cancelled';
          o.refunds++;
          return OK;
        }
        return refuse(`order is ${o.status}, cannot be cancelled`);
      });
    },

    async order(id) {
      const o = orders.get(id);
      return o ? { ...o } : null;
    },

    async pendingEffects() {
      return [...pending.values()].map((e) => ({ ...e, data: { ...e.data } }));
    },

    async completeEffect(id, outcome) {
      const e = pending.get(id);
      if (!e) return;
      pending.delete(id);
      if (e.kind !== 'payment.charge') return;
      const o = orders.get(e.from);
      if (!o || o.status !== 'placed') return;
      o.status = outcome === 'ok' ? 'paid' : 'failed';
    },
  };
}

